### Inject a patch (preferred — synchronous, no debounce)

```js
(() => {
  const patch = `#N canvas;
#X obj 100 100 metro 1000 1;
#X obj 100 200 button;
#X connect 0 0 1 0;
`;
  window.__patchnet.graph.deserialize(patch);
  return { ok: true, nodes: window.__patchnet.graph.nodes.size };
})()
```

`graph.deserialize(text)` rebuilds the graph synchronously and re-renders the text panel as a side effect — no event dispatch, no debounce wait, no race with patchNet's startup render-out path. This is the right path for tests.

Notes:
- The trailing `\n` is required — parser is line-oriented.
- `metro 1000 1` (hidden second arg = 1) starts the metro on load. Without it, you'd need to click the metro to start, which goes through the drag/select layer (see Pitfalls).
- Throws synchronously on unknown object type (e.g. `Error: Unknown object type: doesNotExist`) — wrap in try/catch if you want to assert on the error.
- A few common Pure-Data names don't exist in patchNet — `print`, `number` are not registered. Use `comment` or `float` (and check `objectDefs.ts` if unsure).

