### Object Implementation
Each PD object type has a JavaScript class:

```javascript
// Simplified pd.js object pattern
class OscTilde {
  constructor(args) {
    this.freq = args[0] || 440;
    this.phase = 0;
  }

  dspTick(inBuffers, outBuffers, blockSize, sampleRate) {
    const freq = this.freq;
    const out = outBuffers[0];
    for (let i = 0; i < blockSize; i++) {
      out[i] = Math.sin(this.phase * 2 * Math.PI);
      this.phase = (this.phase + freq / sampleRate) % 1;
    }
  }

  message(inlet, msg) {
    if (inlet === 0) this.freq = msg[0]; // set frequency
  }
}
```

The critical method is `dspTick()` — called every audio block (typically 128 samples) by ScriptProcessorNode's `onaudioprocess` callback. This is called synchronously on the audio thread, blocking main thread execution.

### Message Passing
Control-rate messages travel synchronously through the object graph via direct JavaScript function calls:

```javascript
// When metro fires:
metro.outletObjects[0].forEach(connected => {
  connected.obj.message(connected.inlet, ['bang']);
});
```

This is a pure push model: when an object has output to send, it directly calls the `message()` method on all connected downstream objects.

